t(20, 715, """The words of AI, for the sake of scholarly honesty..

The landscape of the Arabian Peninsula in the rainy ages was completely different from what we see today; it was a "green paradise" teeming with aquatic and land life.
Here are the details of that historical scene based on geological and archaeological evidence:

* Flowing, deep rivers: the Arabian Peninsula contained a wide network of freshwater rivers. Among the most famous is the ancient "Wadi al-Rummah river," which crossed the peninsula from its west to its east.
* Huge lakes: scientists discovered traces of more than ten thousand ancient lakes spread across the region. Even the present-day "Empty Quarter" contained wide freshwater lakes and swamps.
* Forests and green savanna: there were no sand dunes as we know them, but vast green meadows and dense forests resembling the forests found today in Central Africa and India.
* Astonishing wildlife: the peninsula was home to animals we now see only in the African jungles, such as:
* Elephants and giraffes.
  * Hippopotamuses and crocodiles that lived in the rivers and lakes.
  * Lions, tigers and wolves that roamed the Arabian savanna.
* Regular rains: rain fell abundantly throughout the seasons of the year, not only in winter, which made the region then the most suitable place for human and animal life.

This scientific description matches astonishingly the Prophetic hadith: "The Hour will not come until the land of the Arabs returns to meadows and rivers," which scientists see as a reference to natural climatic cycles the earth goes through.""")

