t(23, 225, """While reflecting a little while ago on a verse of our Mighty Lord's Book, the scholars of tafsir, may Allah Most High have mercy on them, said about the words of Allah Most High, holy are His names and attributes, about (the judgment) in the words of our Mighty Lord:
"Those are the ones to whom We gave the Book, judgment and prophethood. So if these disbelieve in it, We have entrusted it to a people who are not disbelievers in it"

Yet the prophets other than the Prophet David, the Prophet Solomon and our Prophet Muhammad ﷺ did not rule as rulers .. The scholars of tafsir said that judgment here means wisdom; others said judgment is deciding between people, and this did not happen with all the prophets. How many a prophet was mocked by his people, and the punishment came to them, and they did not accept their prophet judging between them even in the simplest matters; rather they despised their prophets and wronged them. Another view is that the judgment given to the prophets was understanding and knowledge of the Book, that is, fiqh.

What I want to say about the meaning of "judgment" in the noble verse concerning the prophets, and the knowledge belongs to Allah our Lord alone, is my own understanding that I did not read anywhere, like everything I write by the grace of Allah Most High: the judgment in the noble verse is not fiqh, nor is it wisdom, for the Qur'an is precise in its vocabulary and wording; nor is it judging between litigants, because that did not happen for most of the prophets. Judgment has a deeper meaning than that.

To understand the depth of the meaning of the noble Qur'anic word (judgment) concerning the prophets, we must trace the lives of the prophets, peace be upon them, and look at what recurs in their outcomes, upon them be prayers and peace, where we find the following matter: deliverance from dangers.. When Allah Most High gives the prophets the power to call on Him, and their prayer is answered at once, this is a great dominion on earth. This is the highest and greatest form of dominion and rule on earth.

Look at the dominion of Moses, peace be upon him, that rule supported from heaven, and the earthly dominion of Pharaoh: which of them was truly ruler and king on earth?! Clearly it was the Prophet of Allah Moses, peace be upon him.

Look at the dominion of the Prophet of Allah Salih over his mighty people. Who triumphed and was saved in the end? Was it not the Prophet of Allah Salih, peace be upon him?

Look at the deliverance of the Prophet of Allah Jesus from the cross? Who then was the ruler in truth? The Roman governor or the Prophet of Allah Jesus?

Look at the Prophet of Allah Abraham facing Nimrod, who ruled an empire at that time! Who was the real ruler on earth whom Allah supported in what he willed? Was Nimrod's will fulfilled, or the will of the Prophet of Allah Abraham, peace be upon him?

Look at the might, strength and dominance of Quraysh for years over the Arabs of the Peninsula? Who triumphed in the end? Was it not our Prophet ﷺ and his family?

From here we clearly understand that the prophets were real kings and real rulers on earth. Rulers in truth, not metaphorically! What are the marks of a ruler? That when he commands the means, the means obey him. But the prophets, upon them be prayers and peace, were given the response of heaven, so we are faced with two models: an earthly ruler a thousand times weaker than the ruler surely supported from heaven! So who was the real ruler?!

So when our Mighty Lord, holy are His names and attributes, magnified in the heavens and the earth, and greatly exalted, says that He gave the prophets judgment, that judgment was not wisdom, nor fiqh in religion, nor sitting in a council to judge between two quarreling brothers. The matter was not that simple in meaning; rather it is real rule. A rule supported from heaven, against an earthly rule by earthly means. The strange thing is that people notice earthly rule resting on the very weak, trivial means of the earth, and do not notice the rule supported by heaven that was granted to the prophets!

By this analogy comes the promise of Allah Most High to His friends who are not prophets, that no fear shall be upon them nor shall they grieve. That is why I mentioned long ago that the answered prayer is the greatest representation of the meaning of rule and dominion on earth. Whoever's relationship with the Mighty King, holy are His names, is a true relationship of servitude to the King, is crowned ruler and real king on earth; the whole world submits to him without palace or army, because all power belongs to Allah, not to the means.""")

