t(32, 1445, """The relationship of poultry to domestication and the absence of resolve! The post speaks about adopting it as food, repeating it, eating much of it, or eating it once its harm is proven.

The free bird, in its open, unrestricted movement, resembles sheep, goats and camels, and shepherds have known this since before the era of the Prophet ﷺ and his family. So the free bird in the air is like the free livestock on earth, the camels and sheep that run and move, and is not confined like the confinement of the chicken, whose chest tightens from confinement; and this is among what modern science says. So does eating much chicken really have an effect on human behavior?!

Ibn Taymiyyah, may Allah Most High have mercy on him, says in a long passage on how a person is affected by what he eats, whose meaning I can summarize as follows: "Food turns into part of a person's substance," so if the food carries certain traits, those traits pass into the person's soul and body.

Ibn Taymiyyah cited an agreed-upon hadith that shows the effect of mixing with animals on the soul. The hadith: he ﷺ said: "Pride and arrogance are among the owners of camels, and tranquility and dignity among the people of sheep."

Point of inference: Ibn Taymiyyah holds that if merely mixing with these animals and herding them passes on these traits, then feeding on their meat, which turns into part of the human body, has a stronger and more penetrating effect.

The command to perform ablution after camel meat
Ibn Taymiyyah cited the hadiths that command ablution after camel meat, not sheep, to explain the "nutritional" effect of camels.

The hadith: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: "Shall we perform ablution after camel meat?" He said: "Yes, perform ablution after camel meat," and he was asked about sheep meat and said: "No."

Point of inference: Ibn Taymiyyah mentioned that camels were created from devils (meaning there is skittishness and devilry in them), and that eating their meat passes on a devilish force, rancor and hardness of heart, so the Sharia commanded ablution after it to extinguish that force with water, as fire is extinguished with ablution.

The prohibition of eating animals with fangs and talons""")

t(33, 60, """The scholars linked the prohibition of eating predatory animals with acquiring their aggressive nature.
The hadith: "The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade every predator with fangs and every bird with talons."
Point of inference: Ibn al-Qayyim and Ibn Taymiyyah mentioned that the wisdom of the prohibition is that one who feeds on them may acquire "predatory force," which passes on the character of transgression, aggression and savagery.

The hadith on the prohibition of pork
Although the prohibition came explicitly in the Qur'an, Ibn Taymiyyah indicated that the reason lies in the foulness of what it eats, which is reflected in the nature of one who eats it as cuckoldry and loss of jealousy, by analogy with the Prophetic rule of avoiding foul things.""")

